      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:09 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: May I ask this question on that point"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robbins",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0000-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the statements taken in violation of Miranda cannot be used in the direct case of the government. That's the penalty for Miranda, and if that happens, you get the statement struck in the direct case for the government."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What about the order of a trial judge in a civil case who orders the witness held in contempt and confined unless he testifies, and -- and there's a valid Fifth Amendment privilege that the judge is overlooking? No Fifth Amendment violation there?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0001-0001",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: There is a -- there is a -- there -- what there is is there is an ongoing interference with the Fifth Amendment right that the courts will vindicate, but there isn't a complete constitutional violation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Paz",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0001-0002",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PAZ: There may be a violation, and -- and I would agree that most likely if -- if it was in violation of Miranda, there would be -- there would be no -- it would not be admitted into a criminal case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Except that my hypothetical was not Miranda. My hypothetical was that he was coerced in some fashion other than the failure to give a Miranda warning, and short of beating with a rubber hose."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Paz",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PAZ: I would disagree with you on one point, and that is the -- the language of the amendment talks about a criminal case, and in our brief, we did talk about the meaning, the distinction between a criminal trial and a criminal case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Paz",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PAZ: I believe that there can be some questioning, and I think that the questioning has to be -- even if it's forceful questioning, there must be limits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Robbins",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1444",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2650028de037e72a473c3ce7bf453fd46dfd859246d5beffc93e186474fa4285",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2650028de037-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What you -- what I think it would -- what I think -- what I think it would amount to is continued coercion of a statement which arguably at some threshold, once you cross it, does indeed become too coercive to render the statement admissible."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
